'''''Ludwigia grandiflora''''', the '''water primrose''', is an aquatic plant of the order [[Myrtales]].<ref name="s1">{{cite web |url=http://www.theplantlist.org/tpl1.1/record/tro-23201747 |title=The Plant List: A Working List of All Plant Species |publisher=The Plant List |access-date=27 January 2020 |archive-date=27 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200127143906/http://www.theplantlist.org/tpl1.1/record/tro-23201747 |url-status=live }}</ref>

It is closely related and easily confused with ''[[Ludwigia hexapetala]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://plants.ifas.ufl.edu/wp-content/uploads/files/caip/pdfs/LudwigiaIDGuide.pdf |title=Identification of common aquatic water-primrose species, ''Ludwigia'', in Florida |last=Jacono |first=Colette |date= |website= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200131150233/http://plants.ifas.ufl.edu/wp-content/uploads/files/caip/pdfs/LudwigiaIDGuide.pdf |archive-date=2020-01-31 |access-date=2020-01-27 |url-status=live }}</ref> The two species can be distinguished at a chromosomal level, because ''L.{{nbsp}}grandiflora'' is hexaploid and ''L.{{nbsp}}hexapetala'' is decaploid.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last1=Zardini |first1=Elsa M. |last2=Gu |first2=Hongya |last3=Raven |first3=Peter H. |date=1991 |title=On the separation of two species within the ''Ludwigia uruguayensis'' complex (Onagraceae) |journal=Systematic Botany |volume=16 |issue=2 |pages=242 |doi=10.2307/2419276 |jstor=2419276}}</ref> However, they can be distinguished morphologically. ''L.{{nbsp}}grandiflora'' has villous hairs, smaller flowers and smaller pollen grains.<ref name=":0" /> Some authorities consider that these differences are too slight to consider these different species and so separate these taxa as two varieties or two subspecies.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ward |first=Daniel Bertram |date=2012 |title=New combinations in the Florida Flora III |url=https://biodiversitylibrary.org/page/47218840 |journal=Phytologia |volume=94 |issue=3 |pages=459–485 |via=Biodiversity Heritage Library |access-date=2020-01-28 |archive-date=2020-01-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128091517/https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/47218840 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Nesom |first1=Guy |last2=Kartesz |first2=John |date=2000 |title=Observations on the ''Ludwigia uruguayensis'' complex (Onagraceae) in the United States |journal=Castanea |volume=65 |issue=2 |pages=123–125 |jstor=4034110}}</ref>

== Invasive species ==
''Ludwigia grandiflora'' has been listed on the [[List of invasive alien species of Union concern|List of Invasive Alien Species of Union concern]] since 2016<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://ec.europa.eu/environment/nature/invasivealien/list/index_en.htm |title=List of Invasive Alien Species of Union concern - Environment - European Commission |website=ec.europa.eu |access-date=2020-01-27 |archive-date=2019-12-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191230154209/https://ec.europa.eu/environment/nature/invasivealien/list/index_en.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> and in the United States it is on the [[South Carolina]] State-listed Noxious Weed list.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://plants.usda.gov/java/noxious?rptType=State&statefips=45 |title=South Carolina State Noxious Weeds List {{!}} USDA PLANTS |website=plants.usda.gov |access-date=2020-01-28 |archive-date=2020-09-19 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200919132522/https://plants.usda.gov/java/noxious?rptType=State&statefips=45 |url-status=live }}</ref> It out-competes other plants by forming dense mats at the margins and in ponds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://wiki.bugwood.org/Ludwigia_grandiflora |title=''Ludwigia grandiflora'' - Bugwoodwiki |website=wiki.bugwood.org |access-date=2020-01-28 |archive-date=2020-01-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128091518/https://wiki.bugwood.org/Ludwigia_grandiflora |url-status=live }}</ref> It is introduced to warm temperate areas of [[North America]], [[Japan]] and [[Europe]] and has formed large stable populations, particularly in France.<ref name="DandelotVerlaque2005">{{cite journal |last1=Dandelot |first1=Sophie |last2=Verlaque |first2=Régine |last3=Dutartre |first3=Alain |last4=Cazaubon |first4=Arlette |title=Ecological, dynamic and taxonomic problems due to ''Ludwigia'' (Onagraceae) in France |journal=Hydrobiologia |volume=551 |issue=1 |year=2005 |pages=131–136 |doi=10.1007/s10750-005-4455-0 |s2cid=46339363 }}</ref>
As of summer 2024, the Portuguese city of [[Arganil]] announces measures against the species in its river [[Alva River|Alva]].<ref>https://correiodabeiraserra.sapo.pt/arganil-esta-a-proceder-ao-combate-das-especies-invasoras-no-rio-alva/</ref>
[[File:Ludwigia grandiflora.jpg|thumb|''Ludwigia grandiflora'' found in the Lower Hillsborough River Wilderness Preserve in Florida.]]

== Life history ==
''Ludwigia grandiflora'' can, and does, produce viable seed, but it is also highly effective at vegetative reproduction and apparently recruitment of new plants from seed is low.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Okada |first1=Miki |last2=Grewell |first2=Brenda J. |last3=Jasieniuk |first3=Marie |date=2009-10-01 |title=Clonal spread of invasive ''Ludwigia hexapetala'' and ''L. grandiflora'' in freshwater wetlands of California |journal=Aquatic Botany |language=en |volume=91 |issue=3 |pages=123–129 |doi=10.1016/j.aquabot.2009.03.006|bibcode=2009AqBot..91..123O }}</ref> The large showy flowers attract a wide variety of insects. A study in Belgium, where ''L.{{nbsp}}grandiflora'' is introduced, showed that the flowers are visited by a wide variety of insects including bees, [[Lepidoptera]], beetles and hoverflies.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Stiers |first1=Iris |last2=Triest |first2=Ludwig |date=2017-10-01 |title=Low interspecific pollen transfer between invasive aquatic ''Ludwigia grandiflora'' and native co-flowering plants |journal=Biological Invasions |language=en |volume=19 |issue=10 |pages=2913–2925 |doi=10.1007/s10530-017-1494-1 |bibcode=2017BiInv..19.2913S |s2cid=7022310 |url=https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/60d358006d338474554a9c1abd287151f58950ce |access-date=2020-02-03 |archive-date=2023-07-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720225706/https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Low-interspecific-pollen-transfer-between-invasive-Stiers-Triest/60d358006d338474554a9c1abd287151f58950ce |url-status=live }}</ref>

